

                     Flying Muffin Enterprises 
                              presents

                             F3 Missile
                              Ver. 1.0
                           
                 Falcon Missile Parameter Adjuster

   
  F3MSL.EXE will patch Falcon (ver 3.04-3.05), MiG (ver 1.03-1.04) 
  and Hornet (ver 1.02) to make missiles act more realistically. 
  Version 1.0 offers support only for the shoulder-launched 
  weapons (SA-7s and Stingers).
  
  THIS PROGRAM ALTERS THE FALCON SERIES EXECUTABLE FILES; BACK UP 
  FALCON3.EXE / FALCONCD.EXE, MIG29.EXE / MIGCD.EXE, and HORNET.EXE 
  / HORNETCD.EXE BEFORE RUNNING THIS PROGRAM.
                             
  To use it, simply copy the file F3MSL.EXE into your Falcon 
  directory and type "F3MSL <Enter>." 
               
  Overview:
   
   If there's one aspect of F3 that sim pilots have complained 
   about, it would have to be the SA-7. There are three good 
   reasons why the complaint is valid:
   
   * If flies too fast. In Falcon, it has a maximum speed of Mach 3 
     or more, whereas the latest variant of the real SA-7 maxes out 
     well under Mach 2.
     
   * It flies too far. An SA-7 in Falcon could fly over 22 miles 
     before running out of fuel. The actual SA-7 has a range of 2-3 
     miles, depending on the version.
   
   * It blows up too good. The warhead on an SA-7 is between 5.5 
     and 7 pounds. It's difficult to determine the actual modeled 
     size of the warhead in Falcon, but based on the warhead sizes 
     of the other missiles, it must be at least 12 pounds. (What 
     really makes it dangerous is the fuze: it's set to go off very 
     close to the center of its target, where you get the full 
     brunt of the blast. Other missiles in Falcon use varying 
     degrees of proximity fuzing, so you only get some of their 
     fragments. While the SA-7 does actually use impact and graze 
     fuzing, the *center* of an aircraft is not what it hits before 
     it explodes!)
   
   F3 Missile addresses all of these items. Indeed, when you first 
   use the program, you might find the missiles too "easy." Well, 
   they're now modeled realistically. The simple fact is, if you're 
   in a jet that's flying at 650 knots and someone on the ground 
   shoots a missile at you, you're going to be two miles away from 
   him in about 12 seconds. In that same 12 seconds the missile is 
   spooling up to speed. It might well overtake you at the two mile 
   mark, if you don't take any evasive actions. But that's not 
   likely. Normally, just kicking in the burners (since we're 
   trying to run away, not lose this IR-homing missile) and 
   increasing altitude is enough.
   
  The Missiles:
   
   F3 Missile models three variants of the SA-7 for Falcon and 
   Hornet. For MiG, it uses those same three models but calls them 
   different names.
   
   SA-7A (Strela-2A) and FIM-43 (Redeye):

     You are virtually invulnerable to this missile if you are 
     above 8500 feet, no matter what your speed. At lower 
     altitudes, a speed of 650 knots or above will keep you out of 
     trouble for the most part. Even if you do manage to get hit by 
     one, you'll probably survive. If you get hit by several, 
     you'll go down, but you shouldn't have been entrusted with a 
     combat aircraft anyway! 

   SA-7B (Strela-2M) and FIM-92A (Stinger):   

     This missile is only effective at altitudes under 11,000 feet, 
     and even then only if you are flying quite slowly. At any 
     altitude, you can usually outrun it, dependent upon your 
     initial speed when it's launched. If you're low and slow, 
     it'll take some finesse to get away. It's quite a bit faster 
     than the SA-7A.
     
     If you do get hit, the angle off is quite important: a strike 
     right up the tailpipe will almost certainly knock out your 
     engine, while a glancing blow will normally do only minimal 
     damage. Most high-altitude hits are glancing blows, since the 
     missile is angled up trying to reach you. It's the tail 
     chases, at low altitude, which are most dangerous.
         
   SA-7B/A (Strela-2M/A) and FIM-92A/P (Stinger-POST):
   
     This is the most dangerous of the bunch, but still nothing 
     compared to the Falcon defaults. The biggest improvements over 
     the SA-7B are the larger warhead and a marked improvement in 
     acceleration.
   
  F3 Missile and Comms Use:

   It is recommended that all systems involved (caller and 
   answerers) be consistent in their choice of missile variant.
   
